course: dictionary
lesson: 7337
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. A dictionary lists them as 1), 2) and 3). The sentences around a word, its context, show you which meaning the writer chose. «Sugar cane plantations in Brazil and the Caribbean were worked mainly by enslaved Africans.» Here a plantation is a large farm where one crop is grown.
explain: Look for clues. «Zoos outside Australia grow eucalyptus plantations» is about trees, so a plantation here is an area where many trees are planted in rows. Ask yourself: what would fit here? Then swap your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Some words carry a painful history. «Enslaved people worked on plantations without pay or freedom» tells you that, in the past, many plantations were built on forced, unpaid work. This was a serious wrong. When a word has a sad past, read the whole sentence carefully and take the history seriously.
fact: A plantation is a large farm, often in a hot country, where one crop such as cotton, sugar, tea, coffee or rubber is grown.
fact: A plantation can also be an area where many trees have been planted in rows, for example for wood.
fact: In history, plantations in the Americas used enslaved African people who were forced to work without pay.
fact: Tea plantations in India and Sri Lanka, and palm oil plantations, show how farming can change land and forests.
fact: The Plantation of Ulster was a time when land in Ireland was taken and given to settlers from Britain.
exercise: «Sugar cane plantations in Brazil and the Caribbean were worked mainly by enslaved Africans.» In this sentence, what is a plantation? | A park with a lake | *A large farm where one crop is grown | A small garden for growing flowers | A school for young farmers
exercise: «Zoos outside Australia grow eucalyptus plantations.» What does plantation mean here? | *An area where many trees have been planted in rows | A kind of leaf | A type of koala | A busy market in a town
exercise: Leo says a plantation is always a place where people are forced to work. What is the best reply? | No, it is a word for a fish | No, it is always a school | Yes, that is its only meaning | *Not always: it can also mean a farm growing one crop, or rows of planted trees
test: «Enslaved people worked on plantations without pay or freedom.» What does this sentence tell you about plantations in history? | *They were places of forced, unpaid work | They were parks with lakes and bridges | They were places where people went on holiday | They were schools for young children
test: Which meaning fits «palm oil plantations» in a geography lesson? | A group of friends who play in the park | A market where food is sold | A kind of tree that grows only in cold places | *Large areas of farmland where one crop is grown
exam: What is the best way to work out the meaning of an unknown word? | Only use the first meaning in the dictionary, always | Guess the first meaning you think of and never check it | *Read the words around it, then swap your meaning into the sentence and check it still makes sense | Skip the sentence and forget the word
